the metropolitan police have announced  that their investigation into alleged  coveted breaches at whitehall and in  downing street during the lockdowns has  concluded the met has said that in total  detectives have made 126 referrals for  fixed penalty notices to the acro  criminal records office for breaches of  covid 19 regulations the referrals refer  to events on eight separate dates the  20th of may 2020 the 18th and 19th of  june 2020 the 13th of november 2020 the  17th and 18th of december 2020 the 14th  of january last year and the 16th of  april last year the met says that of the  referrals all will result in a fixed  penalty notice being issued it says the  breakdown of recipients is that uh they  are 53 men and 73 women and some people  have received more than one fixed  penalty notice the met says that it will  not be referring or conf releasing or  confirming the identity of anyone  released in the investigation or  providing any further details of its  findings in line with the approach that  it has taken from the pandemic and the  met goes on to say our position from  early on in the pandemic was that we  would not routinely investigate historic  breaches of covid regulations this was  for two reasons first that we could not  retrospectively engage and inform those  involved that they were breaching the  rules an important step in our policing  strategy around covid and that secondly  as these were summary only offenses we  did not judge it a proportionate use of  officers time and the met goes on to say  we did recognize there might be some  exceptions in this approach and we set  out at the time the criteria that would  be need for us to be met to consider a  retrospective investigation and the met  goes on to say uh those included that  there was evidence that those involved  uh should have known that they were  breaching the regulations of what they  knew was doing was an offense when not  investigating would significantly  undermine the legitimacy of the law and  where there was little ambiguity around  the absence of any reasonable defense so  the met investigation has tied up we can  speak now to our political correspondent  tomorrow cohen for her reaction there  tomorrow what do you make of this  morning ian well big news from  westminster about that partygate  investigation which has really dominated  the past few months and at times looked  as if it was putting boris johnson's  premiership in jeopardy it's concluded  there have been 126  penalty notices issued as far as we know  the prime minister has only got that one  from the beginning we do not know that  he's had any more since then and downing  street did tell us that we would be told  if he had  of those 126 they cover eight different  events one being that birthday party  that um  that that the prime minister had with it  with the sing-along interestingly  none of the dates uh include so that the  20th of may which was the event  including the email invite to his  socially distanced drinks is included on  that list but we  don't believe thus far the prime  minister has received a penalty notice  over that just taking in this very long  statement they tell us  how many officers worked on it they  worked through hundreds 345 documents  and over 500 photographs and they  explained in detail as you were setting  out there why they decided to  investigate this breach retrospectively  having not done so before because they  they did so in cases where they feel it  would significantly undermine the  legitimacy of the law not to have done  so so partygates concluded  if the prime minister only gets one fine  that will put him in a significantly  better position regarding his mps than  it would do if he received more than  that but what will now happen is that we  will get the sue grey report into all  the details of what happened at those  events and we'll be waiting for updates  from the cabinet office about when that  will happen
 

